### What is the In-Sync Replica set (ISR)?

Each partition has one leader and several follower replicas. The ISR is the set of replicas fully caught up with the leader. When a follower falls too far behind, it is removed from the ISR. When it catches up, it rejoins.

```java
package com.bank.replication;

public class IsrExplained {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Partition 0 (replication factor 3):");
        System.out.println("  Broker 1: LEADER  (ISR)");
        System.out.println("  Broker 2: follower (ISR) -- caught up");
        System.out.println("  Broker 3: follower (ISR) -- caught up");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("After Broker 3 falls behind:");
        System.out.println("  Broker 1: LEADER  (ISR)");
        System.out.println("  Broker 2: follower (ISR)");
        System.out.println("  Broker 3: follower (OUT of ISR)");
        System.out.println("  ISR size: 2");
    }
}
```

```text
Partition 0 (replication factor 3):
  Broker 1: LEADER  (ISR)
  Broker 2: follower (ISR) -- caught up
  Broker 3: follower (ISR) -- caught up

After Broker 3 falls behind:
  Broker 1: LEADER  (ISR)
  Broker 2: follower (ISR)
  Broker 3: follower (OUT of ISR)
  ISR size: 2
```

### Why does acks=all alone have a gap?

With acks=all the producer waits for every replica in the ISR. But if the ISR shrinks to just the leader, acks=all means acks on one replica. If that leader then fails, an acknowledged record is lost.

```java
package com.bank.replication;

public class AcksAllGap {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("ISR = {Broker1, Broker2, Broker3}:");
        System.out.println("  acks=all means write on 3 replicas -- SAFE");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("ISR shrinks to {Broker1} only:");
        System.out.println("  acks=all means write on 1 replica");
        System.out.println("  Broker1 fails -> acknowledged record LOST!");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("This is the gap min.insync.replicas closes.");
    }
}
```

```text
ISR = {Broker1, Broker2, Broker3}:
  acks=all means write on 3 replicas -- SAFE

ISR shrinks to {Broker1} only:
  acks=all means write on 1 replica
  Broker1 fails -> acknowledged record LOST!

This is the gap min.insync.replicas closes.
```

### How does min.insync.replicas close the gap?

min.insync.replicas tells the broker the minimum ISR size for an acks=all write. If the ISR drops below this number, the broker rejects the write with NotEnoughReplicas.

```java
package com.bank.config;

public class MinIsrConfig {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Configuration:");
        System.out.println("  replication.factor    = 3");
        System.out.println("  min.insync.replicas   = 2");
        System.out.println("  acks                  = all");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("ISR = 3: write succeeds (3 >= 2)");
        System.out.println("ISR = 2: write succeeds (2 >= 2)");
        System.out.println("ISR = 1: write REJECTED (1 < 2)");
        System.out.println("  -> NotEnoughReplicasException");
        System.out.println("  -> Producer retries until ISR recovers");
    }
}
```

```text
Configuration:
  replication.factor    = 3
  min.insync.replicas   = 2
  acks                  = all

ISR = 3: write succeeds (3 >= 2)
ISR = 2: write succeeds (2 >= 2)
ISR = 1: write REJECTED (1 < 2)
  -> NotEnoughReplicasException
  -> Producer retries until ISR recovers
```

### How do you set the configuration?

```bash
# Set at topic level
kafka-configs --alter --topic transfers \
  --add-config min.insync.replicas=2

# Create topic with replication factor
kafka-topics --create --topic transfers \
  --partitions 6 --replication-factor 3
```

```java
package com.bank.producer;

import org.apache.kafka.clients.producer.ProducerConfig;
import java.util.Properties;

public class DurableProducerConfig {
    public static Properties config() {
        Properties props = new Properties();
        props.put(ProducerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, "broker1:9092");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.ACKS_CONFIG, "all");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.ENABLE_IDEMPOTENCE_CONFIG, "true");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.KEY_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG,
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer");
        props.put(ProducerConfig.VALUE_SERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG,
            "org.apache.kafka.common.serialization.StringSerializer");
        return props;
    }
}
```

### What is the durability-availability trade-off? (comparison)

| Config | Broker losses tolerated (writes continue) | Broker losses tolerated (no data loss) | Risk |
|---|---|---|---|
| RF=3, min.isr=1, acks=all | 2 | 0 (ISR can be 1) | Acknowledged data can be lost |
| RF=3, min.isr=2, acks=all | 1 | 1 | Standard safe config |
| RF=3, min.isr=3, acks=all | 0 | 2 | Any broker loss halts writes |

### What are common mistakes?

```java
package com.bank.mistake;

public class MinIsrMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // WRONG: acks=all without min.insync.replicas
        System.out.println("WRONG: acks=all, min.insync.replicas=1 (default)");
        System.out.println("  ISR can shrink to 1 -> acknowledged data at risk");
        System.out.println();

        // CORRECT: pair acks=all with min.insync.replicas=2
        System.out.println("CORRECT: acks=all, min.insync.replicas=2, RF=3");
        System.out.println();

        // WRONG: min.insync.replicas = replication.factor
        System.out.println("WRONG: min.insync.replicas=3, RF=3");
        System.out.println("  One broker loss halts ALL writes -- too fragile");
        System.out.println();

        // WRONG: min.insync.replicas with acks=1
        System.out.println("WRONG: min.insync.replicas=2 but acks=1");
        System.out.println("  acks=1 never checks ISR size -- setting is useless");
    }
}
```

```text
WRONG: acks=all, min.insync.replicas=1 (default)
  ISR can shrink to 1 -> acknowledged data at risk

CORRECT: acks=all, min.insync.replicas=2, RF=3

WRONG: min.insync.replicas=3, RF=3
  One broker loss halts ALL writes -- too fragile

WRONG: min.insync.replicas=2 but acks=1
  acks=1 never checks ISR size -- setting is useless
```

### How do you monitor ISR health?

Monitoring the ISR set is critical because a shrinking ISR means replicas are falling behind and durability is weakening. Alert on `UnderReplicatedPartitions` and `UnderMinIsrPartitionCount` JMX metrics. A partition where the ISR set drops below `min.insync.replicas` will reject writes if `acks=all`, causing producer exceptions.

```java
package com.bank.kafka;

public class IsrMonitoring {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("ISR monitoring checklist:");
        System.out.println("  JMX: kafka.server:type=ReplicaManager,name=UnderReplicatedPartitions");
        System.out.println("    Alert if > 0 for more than 5 minutes");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  JMX: kafka.server:type=ReplicaManager,name=UnderMinIsrPartitionCount");
        System.out.println("    Alert immediately: writes will fail with acks=all");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  CLI: kafka-topics.sh --describe --under-replicated-partitions");
        System.out.println("    Shows which partitions have shrunk ISR");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Grafana dashboard: ISR size per partition over time");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
ISR monitoring checklist:
  JMX: kafka.server:type=ReplicaManager,name=UnderReplicatedPartitions
    Alert if > 0 for more than 5 minutes

  JMX: kafka.server:type=ReplicaManager,name=UnderMinIsrPartitionCount
    Alert immediately: writes will fail with acks=all

  CLI: kafka-topics.sh --describe --under-replicated-partitions
    Shows which partitions have shrunk ISR

  Grafana dashboard: ISR size per partition over time
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The two settings only deliver strong durability when used together. min.insync.replicas without acks=all is useless. Replication factor must be strictly greater than min.insync.replicas to tolerate a broker loss while still accepting writes. This is the deliberate choice to refuse data rather than risk losing it.
